    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the proximal tibia. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Surgical resection alone", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 54-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal femur. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["Wide surgical resection", "Denosumab therapy", "Systemic chemotherapy", "High-dose radiation therapy", "Radiofrequency ablation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 14-year-old child sustains a minor ankle sprain. Radiographs are negative for fracture but show an incidental, 2 cm, eccentric, bubbly, cortically based radiolucent lesion with a thin sclerotic margin in the distal tibial metaphysis. The patient has no pain directly over the lesion. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Curettage and bone grafting", "Immediate core needle biopsy", "MRI of the entire tibia", "Prophylactic internal fixation", "Reassurance and observation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This classic description is a Non-Ossifying Fibroma (NOF) or Fibrous Cortical Defect. These are asymptomatic, developmental defects seen in up to 30-40% of normal children. They invariably resolve/ossify spontaneously over time and require only observation unless they are large enough (>50% of bone diameter) to pose a risk of pathologic fracture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old presents with a painful, enlarging mass around the distal femur. Radiographs reveal a destructive, mixed lytic and blastic lesion in the metaphysis, with a ''sunburst'' periosteal reaction and a Codman triangle. A biopsy shows malignant cells producing unmineralized osteoid. Which of the following is the standard treatment protocol?', '["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy, surgical resection, and adjuvant chemotherapy", "Surgical resection alone", "Curettage, phenol adjuvant, and cementation", "Neoadjuvant radiation and chemotherapy without surgery", "Primary radiation therapy followed by resection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteosarcoma is the most common primary bone malignancy in adolescents. It typically occurs in the metaphysis of long bones. Histologic hallmark is the production of osteoid by malignant cells. Standard treatment is neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, wide surgical resection (limb salvage), followed by adjuvant chemotherapy based on tumor necrosis rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "Diffuse periosteal uptake", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 22-year-old male presents with deep, aching pain in the proximal femur that is characteristically worse at night. The pain is completely relieved by ibuprofen. Radiographs show a small (< 1.5 cm) radiolucent nidus surrounded by dense reactive sclerosis. What is the most appropriate definitive management if symptoms become refractory?', '["Wide surgical resection", "Intravenous bisphosphonates", "Radiation therapy", "Curettage and bone grafting", "Radiofrequency ablation (RFA)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osteoid osteoma classically presents with night pain relieved by NSAIDs due to high prostaglandin (PGE2) production by the tumor nidus. CT is the best imaging modality to visualize the nidus. If medical management fails or is poorly tolerated, percutaneous radiofrequency ablation (RFA) is the treatment of choice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["McCune-Albright syndrome", "Maffucci syndrome", "Neurofibromatosis type 1", "Ollier disease", "Multiple hereditary exostoses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal radius. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Radiation therapy", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Amputation", "Denosumab", "Bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal radius. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.');
